<div class="data-source">
<p class="citation">Lekhnitskii, S. G. (1981). Theory of Elasticity of an Anisotropic Body. Mir Publishers, Moscow.</p>
<p><span class="label">Read in</span> Table 6, p. 64; Section 31, Eqs. (31.1), (31.17) and (31.18), Tables 8, 9 and 10, pp. 170-175 <span class="badge ok">checked against the source</span></p>
<p><span class="label">Transcribed from</span> the book (scanned PDF), read on the images of the pages</p>
<p class="note">An infinite plate with an elliptic hole of semi-axes a &gt;= b, pulled by a uniform stress p at infinity, in generalized plane stress. phi is the angle of the pull with the major axis of the ellipse, psi the angle of the principal direction of elasticity of the larger Young modulus with that axis, both counted in the same sense (Fig. 46 of the book). The tables give the largest tensile stress and the largest compressive stress (as a positive number) along the edge of the hole, divided by p, for the ratio a/b = 2 and the three-ply birch plywood of Table 6; the book says that two decimals are kept. The moduli of Table 6 are printed as multiples of 10^5 kgf/cm^2 and are stored as these multiples: only their ratios enter.</p>
</div>
